A1. This is what drove the children away from home. ~ CHAUFFEUR
A9. This bud expects to come out next fall. ~ ASTER
A14. A mill could make millions with these. ~ IONS
A15. This made a good clean up in the spring. ~ RAKE
A16. As a matter of form we will let you figure this out yourself. ~ SHAPE
A17. You can take your pick for this—either from a shell or a cell. ~ NUT
A18. This makes wonderful preserves. ~ FOREST
A20. Where Mike McGinn is. ~ ERIN
A21. A bird with a big bill. (The kind that brought the Lindbergh baby.) (abbr.) ~ DR
A22. This is absolutely necessary. ~ MUST
A23. When one orders a thick steak and the waiter puts this kind of a thing over, it makes one hot. ~ TABASCO
A25. We put this in, in case you lose your directions. ~ NORTH
A26. Your eraser might explain this. ~ SMUT
A27. Woolworth's family ties. ~ RAYON
A29. The end of the fall. ~ THUMP
A33. A little before. ~ ERE
A34. This is wits' end. ~ ITS
A37. The warmest part of hotels. ~ HOT
A39. An exclamation that comes from a backward leap. ~ POH
A40. Try this to get your lighter going. ~ LI
A41. This one had the highest score of the four. ~ SOPRANO
A42. The end of leisure and the beginning of responsibility. ~ RE
A43. There is quite a field for improvement in this. ~ LEA
A45. The call of the road. ~ HEY
A46. These are found in menageries. ~ MEN
A47. Be careful—this might start a panic. ~ PAN
A48. This was furnished by her late husband. ~ ALIBI
A50. The first thing she said after she got her ring. ~ HELLO
A52. This is one in a hundred. ~ UNIT
A55. Have you heard this one? ~ STORY
A57. The town in Germany that Schmeling comes from. ~ COLOGNE
A60. This made it hot for China. ~ KILN
A61. Take this away and it leaves a broken he-art. ~ HE
A62. Where mosquitoes never go. ~ AWAY
A63. Erase! ~ DELETE
A65. This is where dad's next meal will probably come from. ~ CAN
A66. You might call this a number of things. ~ SEVEN
A68. Wouldn't this make you tired? ~ TOIL
A69. Always a good sport. ~ POLO
A70. To swap. ~ TRADE
A71. This is what they spent in Paris. ~ HONEYMOON

D1. A slippery maid. ~ CINDERELLA
D2. We will give you some time for this. ~ HOUR
D3. A member of the working class. ~ ANT
D4. Damp spot (abbr.) ~ US
D5. This helps turn the leaves in the book of nature. ~ FROST
D6. A happy landing place. ~ EARTH
D7. This accompanied "Mary Lou" at the beach party. ~ UKE
D8. Silent members of the staff. ~ RESTS
D9. This has always been in fashion. ~ AS
D10. This is a case where you can't see the point. ~ SHEATH
D11. These make the best hands in the deck. ~ TARS
D12. The poem the publisher did not return. ~ EPIC
D13. Where the United States end. ~ RENO
D18. What dad is doing for a little change for the family. ~ FURNISHING
D19. Something that Sandy has on his mind. ~ TAM
D22. This sounds rather bossy. ~ MOO
D24. A good opening for a tailor shop. ~ BUTTONHOLE
D25. A funny Bill that was introduced some time ago. ~ NYE
D28. The spirit of "The Tempest." ~ ARIEL
D30. The way little Lindy will grow. ~ UP
D31. Ethical. ~ MORAL
D32. A cow sitting on a thistle singing like a bird. ~ PHENOMENON
D35. This is now at the foot, with some training it might reach the head. ~ TOE
D36. A Hale fellow in revolutionary days. ~ SPY
D37. Smoking cured this one. ~ HAM
D38. A popular figure with the waiters. ~ ONE
D44. This fellow has been identified by having only three toes. ~ AI
D47. What to do to keep a steady job. ~ PLY
D49. This kind of thing has been kept from sinking for a long time. ~ BUOYED
D51. An eagle that has earned his way into puzzledom. ~ ERN
D53. This came from Indiana. ~ IND
D54. These are biting well this season. ~ TEETH
D55. Believe it or not this is some yarn. ~ SKEIN
D56. Whatyoucallit. ~ TITLE
D57. From the stars to the sub. ~ CAST
D58. For vertical reasons we omitted the end of this tower. ~ OWER
D59. This was discharged last year. ~ LAVA
D61. A shining light in brilliant circles. ~ HALO
D64. A card game. ~ LOO
D65. This one is with bill in a petty company. ~ COO
D67. The northeast part of Nevada. ~ NE
D69. This goes on from noon till midnight. ~ PM


